= Introduction<intro>

== Motivation
  
Both artifical intelligence and deep learning architectures are currently undergoing a rapid development with ever-increasing optimization efforts. With the introduction of transformer models user-oriented applications to generate text, audio and videos or to identify everday sequences which are featuring trainable logic. Furthermore, they have been declared as all-rounder tools, capeable of slowly rendering the first jobs obsolete. This begs the question if they are really able to work as a multi-tool or if they just profit from the large range of training data and if there will be some kind of bias due to coarse data with less depth and no focus on a special task like emotion detection. 

From an early stage of emotion research, psychological approaches have shown fixed behavioral patterns when showing emotions and sentiments including face and voice actions regarding semantic and syntax. Basic math and machine learning algorithms were able to classify some information and identify emotion. However neural networks are bringing more performance, better results in less time. The introduced transformers are infamous for their contextual understanding as well as very good and consistent outcomes from the given tasks.

This leads to the question if already developed applications like ChatGPT or general approaches based on deep learning are able to recognize emotion from video, audio and text in the context of interviews with a static view-angle and real-life excerpts. The goal of this thesis is to compare state of the art models and architectures mostly focused on transformers which are present in every modal. Further multimodal emotion detection is a topic to analyze different feature fusion models and wether the different labels from the different architectures might lead to a more accurate results than a single modal outcome. This topic covers basic voting strategies up to further more complex architectures which are using the logits, kernels or other raw output from the models. A comparison can be done to show if the effort is necessary for an accurate result or if given basic methods are nearly as good or even better. These points sequels into an assumption that bimodal emotion classifcation can be more efficient and asymptotically accurate. Generally looking at unimodal classification which might show that one of the modals might have a small impact on the results but not an crucial one. Intuitively this can be emotion classification with features from text due to the impact of irony or other vocal features bringing an ambigious meaning for sentences or words related to their emotion label. The opportunity to ignore one or more modals offers several optimization opportunities as for example one large architecture less is needed or the chosen feature combination method has a reduction in complexity and computation time. 

In general, emotion detection introduces an interesting field of opportunity for research regarding machine-based simulation of human behavior and understanding of interactions related to interviews or conversations. Additionally, after learning from the features, most generative models are able to reproduce emotion alongside the trained information giving perspective in robotics or other forms of real world imitations. Current research is still focused on the general, non-transformer based models like convolution, reccurent networks or even simpler machine learning approaches. Due to limitation from copyright or closed-source projects some architectures have been reproduced but not trained on the original datasets, possibly leading to different accuracys in comparison to the usage of the original model with the pretrained weights. 


== Structure

This work starts by showing and discussing the fundamental, psychological backgrounds in Chapter 2 for the term emotion and which features are necessary to identify them. Furthermore, the classification approaches in machine and deep learning are presented showing their unsupervised and supervised methods for training and feature extraction. At the end of this chapter the datasets for the further implementation parts are introduced. Related works in Chapter 3 are presented to display current research on multi-modal detection and their different methods and learning proceedings. Continuing with the design and implementation of the different supervised models, Chapter 4 presents self-trained and also pretrained models and how to expand as well as finetune their knowledge. In addition the architectures to fuse the information for multi-model emotion detection are shown and different fusion methods are discussed including some kinds of basic scoring and more advanced label combinations. These accomplishments are evaluated in Chapter 5 introducting measurement criteria for reliable and comparable results. Finally, this thesis is summarized and a comperative overview is given to contrast some outcomes from related work concluding with a look on possible future work in emotion detection. 


